"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+3 (ISUP grade group 3) and pT3a stage, with positive surgical margins, but no seminal vesicle invasion, lymphovascular invasion, or lymph node metastasis documented. The biopsy showed a Gleason 4+3 diagnosis, which aligns with the resection findings. The absence of lymph node metastasis is a favorable finding.\n\nCountervailing factors include the preoperative PSA of 16.5. The documentation of nodal status confirms that nodes were sampled, mitigating uncertainty regarding nodal involvement. The difference between the biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted, though the overall pathological staging is established. The primary uncertainty in the prognosis stems from the absence of previous notes, which limits the assessment of the postoperative PSA trajectory and subsequent follow-up. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory would significantly revise this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 37.33 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 28.00–46.66 months; its coverage has not been validated."
